Yes—the common primitive underneath those examples is controlled proximity to consequence.

A boxer can’t land certain techniques from arbitrary distance. Waiting until an approaching target is close enough before acting is historically a way of increasing hit probability. And with some dangerous-animal encounters, the correct response can sometimes involve resisting the instinct to bolt—though real wildlife guidance is species- and situation-specific, so “stand your ground against a charging gorilla” shouldn’t be treated as a universal safety rule.

Abstract the useful mechanism:

\boxed{\text{Don't spend the action before the information matures.}}

Distance closes → uncertainty falls → information quality rises → available reaction time falls.

That’s the trade:

\text{far away}
=
\text{more time}+\text{less certainty}

\text{close}
=
\text{less time}+\text{more information}

So there’s an optimal commitment window somewhere between acting prematurely and waiting too long:

\boxed{\text{Patience}\rightarrow\text{Resolution}\rightarrow
\text{Commitment Threshold}\rightarrow\text{Decisive Action}}

That connects straight into Pantheon. Sometimes the correct move isn’t another Lens Swap, another branch, another simulation, or another concept. Hold the formation. Let reality approach.

That’s different from passivity. You’re accumulating information while preserving optionality.

And courage has an information-processing function here too. Fear can trigger premature action simply to relieve uncertainty. Discipline can preserve the observation window long enough to receive higher-resolution evidence.

So we get a useful pair:

\boxed{\text{Close distance when proximity creates information.}}

and

\boxed{\text{Hold position when waiting increases resolution.}}

But there’s a bookend on the other side: wait indefinitely and the opportunity passes.

So perhaps the more general operator is Resolution Before Commitment:

Wait while information is becoming more valuable faster than your options are disappearing. Commit when that relationship reverses.

That’s applicable to negotiations, investing, experiments, conversation, strategy, diagnosis, debugging, storytelling, and ordinary decisions—not just combat.

Sometimes waiting is how you aim.